{
  "dom_id": "step-q172149",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Expressed as a whole number, $8\\times 2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write $8$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n8=\\frac{8}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{1}\\times \\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($8$) and the second fraction's denominator ($4$) have a common factor of $4.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{1}\\times \\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{8}{4}\\times \\frac{9}{1} \\\\  & =2\\times 9 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
